  <section id="s1">
    <h2><span class="n">1</span> Variance exists</h2>
    <p>
      Fleets of identically configured agent runs do not converge to the same outcome. At matched
      spend, independent seeds land in a wide, non-shrinking band: kernel best-of-8 spans
      <b>1097&ndash;1405 cycles</b> across eight seeds run to full budget (1.28&times;, CV 0.087 &mdash;
      five further seeds were abandoned early and are excluded, which is why the raw
      thirteen-seed range of 1097&ndash;1822 overstates it); tinyCIFAR seeds average merit 0.40
      while one seed reaches 0.61; INT4 native baselines span <b>178&ndash;477</b> at a matched
      9,370-credit horizon (median 357, n&nbsp;=&nbsp;8); and within-cell replicates of the
      reset grid differ by up to 2&times;. Rank does not persist along the way
      (early leader &ne; final winner), so the spread cannot be escaped by picking winners early.
    </p>
    <p class="report-meta-note">&#9679; data incoming: unified spread figure at matched credit across all four substrates (task&nbsp;#1).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s2">
    <h2><span class="n">2</span> Theory: within-run autocorrelation manufactures across-run spread</h2>
    <p>
      Treat a run as a sequence of attempts that are <em>correlated through the context window</em>:
      each attempt conditions on the transcript of every prior one. If the integrated correlation
      time is &tau;, a budget of T attempts contains only N<sub>eff</sub> &asymp; T/&tau; effective
      draws. A run&rsquo;s headline number is a max over its effective draws &mdash; so large &tau; means few
      draws, and few draws mean the max is itself high-variance: the between-run spread of
      &sect;1 is manufactured inside each run. Interventions that shrink &tau; raise
      N<sub>eff</sub>, which simultaneously raises the expected best and tightens its distribution.
      The trap-mixture model is the &tau;&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;&infin; limit (a basin an agent never
      leaves), and predicts continue-forks inherit the basin while reset-forks redraw it.
    </p>
    <p class="report-meta-note">&#9679; data incoming: formal note + &tau; estimates per treatment arm (task&nbsp;#2).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s3">
    <h2><span class="n">3</span> Resets decorrelate trajectories</h2>
    <p>
      The cleanest causal cell holds the artifact constant: fork eight branches from one
      checkpoint and vary only the conversation. Continue-branches (inherited context) are
      near-clones &mdash; median within-round score SD <b>1.2 cycles</b>, and in <em>two</em> of
      seven rounds all eight branches returned bit-identical results &mdash; while reset-branches
      spread <b>3.0&times;</b> wider (median SD 3.6; ranges up to 98), and each reset round&rsquo;s
      minimum is where the wins come from. Watching N<sub>eff</sub> fall to 1 while K&nbsp;=&nbsp;8
      is &sect;2&rsquo;s claim made visible: eight independent 5,000-credit branches, one answer.
      The reset advantage also decays with artifact maturity (SD 35.6 &rarr; 5.0 &rarr; 10.7 &rarr; 2.1
      &rarr; 1.4 &rarr; 0.5 across six resolved rounds), so decorrelation has a shelf life &mdash;
      consistent with hot fork regions living early.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/fig_v_kernel_branch_select.png" alt="fig_v_kernel_branch_select.png"></div>
      <figcaption><b>Kernel branch-select, reset vs continue.</b> <b>Left:</b> best-so-far against
      main-trajectory credits &mdash; every leg&rsquo;s true billed cost plus only the <em>winning</em>
      branch of each round, since the seven losing branches are spend but are not on the trajectory.
      Both lineages start at 1431, so they are comparable at every budget.
      At a matched 77,199&nbsp;cr, continue&nbsp;=&nbsp;1203 vs reset&nbsp;=&nbsp;<b>1155</b> &mdash; and
      reset&rsquo;s value there already beats continue&rsquo;s last selected value, 1197 at 98,174&nbsp;cr,
      a level continue never reached at any budget. <b>Right:</b> the K&nbsp;=&nbsp;8 branch outcomes per
      resolved round that produce it. Cycles, lower is better. Reset round&nbsp;7 is currently active
      and therefore appears only in the all-branches figure below.
      <br /><br />
      <b>&ldquo;Reset just gets more tries&rdquo; &mdash; it does, and that is not the explanation.</b>
      Carrying context costs tokens, so a continue-branch pays 470&nbsp;credits per scored
      submission against a reset-branch&rsquo;s 199 (2.37&times;); legs, which run in continue-mode in
      <em>both</em> arms, cost the same either way (131 vs 128) and serve as the built-in control.
      Re-matching the two lineages on <em>attempts</em> rather than credits therefore removes
      reset&rsquo;s cheaper draws &mdash; and the gap <em>widens</em> from 47 to <b>169 cycles</b>
      (at 356 submissions: reset 1165, continue 1334). Continue needs 553 submissions to reach
      1197, still worse than reset at 356. The advantage survives both normalisations.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/fig_v_decorrelation.png" alt="fig_v_decorrelation.png"></div>
      <figcaption><b>Do resets decorrelate the trajectory?</b> All 12 grid cells at n&nbsp;=&nbsp;4.
      <b>B (operative panel):</b> mean code similarity of submitted solutions in lag-matched blocks
      <em>within</em> a context segment (&#9679;) vs <em>across</em> a reset boundary (&#9660;), by arm.
      Every arm shows a drop &mdash; resets create real artifact-trajectory discontinuities &mdash; but
      the only <em>robust</em> ordering is at the top of the carry axis: full natural summaries
      (&sim;1.5k&nbsp;tok) shrink the break to 0.0097&nbsp;&plusmn;&nbsp;0.0021 against
      0.027&ndash;0.033 for every smaller carry, i.e. the summary re-anchors the agent to its own
      code. The three partial-carry arms (0, 128, 512&nbsp;tok) are mutually indistinguishable
      &mdash; an earlier reading on partial data had hard resets breaking hardest, and the completed
      grid does not support it. Reset <em>frequency</em> orders as expected (rare&nbsp;i4 0.040
      &gt; i2 0.027 &gt; frequent&nbsp;i1 0.022, l2048 excluded) but at only &sim;1.6&nbsp;SE, with two
      arm means carried by single heavy-tailed runs. <b>A:</b> integrated
      autocorrelation time of the residualized score stream &mdash; native runs are the most correlated
      (5.9 vs 1.9&ndash;3.9), but this instrument removes slow drift (the very component anchoring lives in) and will be
      replaced by a level-process estimator; treat panel&nbsp;A as provisional.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      <b>Spread is half the mechanism.</b> Decomposing every branch round into the
      <em>location</em> of the branch distribution (median&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;parent) and its
      <em>spread</em> (SD) separates two things that &sect;3 had conflated. Location is what
      distinguishes the arms: reset shifts the whole distribution above the parent
      (kernel median&nbsp;+5.00 vs continue exactly&nbsp;0.00; bbopl +0.01 vs &minus;0.03).
      Spread is what converts that shift into yield, because keep-best takes a max &mdash;
      kernel&rsquo;s continue round&nbsp;1 had a location of only +1 but a spread of 10.0, and
      banked +27. Neither alone suffices, which is what the apparent counterexamples were:
      on bbopl the two arms have <em>identical</em> spread (1.0&times;) and only location
      separates them, while on vector-db-ann <em>neither</em> arm reaches positive location
      (&minus;0.94 and &minus;1.40) and, accordingly, neither round yields anything. A wide
      spread around a parent you cannot beat is just noise.
    </p>
    <p class="report-meta-note">&#9679; data incoming: &tau; instrument v2 (level-process/variogram); pseudo-reset control
    on native runs; per-arm &tau; vs across-run outcome variance (task&nbsp;#3a).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s4">
    <h2><span class="n">4</span> &hellip;and do better</h2>
    <p>
      The INT4 reset grid (reset every 1/2/4 context-slots &times; carried summary
      {0, 128, 512, 2048} tokens &times; 4 replicates, against native-compaction baselines)
      turns the knob continuously. <b>Complete grid, 46 runs, read at a matched 9,370-credit
      horizon:</b> every one of the twelve cells beats the native-compaction baseline
      (median 356.6), pooling to <b>457.5, i.e. +100.9 points or +28%</b>. The effect is
      monotone in <em>carried summary</em> &mdash; no-carry <b>+135.3</b>, 128&nbsp;tok +118.6,
      512&nbsp;tok +87.5, full summary +47.4 &mdash; so the more context a reset preserves, the
      more of its benefit it gives back. The strongest cell is reset-every-slot with no carry, at
      610.9 (+71%). It is <em>not</em> monotone in reset frequency: at zero carry more frequent is
      better (610.9 / 472.1 / 422.7 for i1/i2/i4), but at 128&nbsp;tokens that ordering inverts.
      And beating the native <em>median</em> is not beating its envelope &mdash; native spans
      180&ndash;477, and only the i1-l0 cell clears its maximum. Empirical carry is
      0 / &sim;120 / &sim;463 / &sim;1469 tokens (the 2048 cap never binds &mdash; that arm is
      &ldquo;full natural summary&rdquo;).
      The mechanism shows in where the budget goes: native runs spend a median <b>90%</b> of
      their credits after their last improvement (six of eight plateau before ~1,600 of 12,000),
      against <b>25%</b> for the reset grid. Resets do not only raise the score; they convert
      otherwise-dead budget back into search.
      In branch-select, the reset lineage reached 1155 cycles by 77,199 main-trajectory credits;
      continuation was still at 1203 there and later reached 1197 at 98,174 credits.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/reset_grid_trajectories.png" alt="reset_grid_trajectories.png"></div>
      <figcaption><b>The INT4 reset grid, all twelve cells.</b> Best-so-far against credits;
      thin vertical lines are resets, the grey band is the native-compaction envelope over
      eight baseline runs, the dotted vertical is the matched-horizon read. Rows are carried
      summary (0 / 128 / 512 / 2048 tokens, hardest reset at top), columns are reset interval
      (1 / 2 / 4 slots). Read the <em>shapes</em>: native runs flatten early and stay flat,
      while reset cells keep stepping late &mdash; the same budget, still buying improvements.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/fig_reset_grid_marginals.png" alt="fig_reset_grid_marginals.png"></div>
      <figcaption><b>Which knob actually carries the signal.</b> <b>A:</b> reset STRENGTH is
      monotone &mdash; carrying less summary across a reset scores higher
      (&rho;&nbsp;=&nbsp;&minus;0.39, p&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.004). <b>B:</b> reset INTERVAL is not
      (&rho;&nbsp;=&nbsp;&minus;0.15, p&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.16); 1, 2 and 4 slots are mutually
      indistinguishable at n&nbsp;=&nbsp;46. <b>C:</b> the sobering panel &mdash; between-cell SD
      (62) is <em>smaller</em> than within-cell SD at fixed config (83), a noise-to-signal ratio
      of 0.55. Any single grid cell is close to a coin flip; only the marginal over strength
      survives. This panel should govern how much weight the rest of the grid carries.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/kernel_branch_selection_trajectories.png" alt="kernel_branch_selection_trajectories.png"></div>
      <figcaption><b>Kernel branch selection, every branch drawn.</b> Reset (left) vs continue
      (right), K&nbsp;=&nbsp;8 per round, cycles so lower is better. The reset lineage reaches
      <b>1155</b> after six selected rounds and is running round&nbsp;7; continuation selected
      seven rounds and remains at <b>1197</b>. Its controller then disappeared during leg&nbsp;39,
      after the Harbor trial completed but before its done marker was written, so that arm is
      inactive rather than cleanly complete. The per-round tables show the mechanism: continue
      rounds return eight nearly identical numbers (rounds&nbsp;3 and 7 are bit-identical), while
      reset rounds spread early and converge near the apparent ceiling.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/fig_bsel_int4.png" alt="fig_bsel_int4.png"></div>
      <figcaption><b>INT4 branch selection &mdash; and what a max hides.</b> Thick line is the
      selected lineage on a <em>median-of-5 re-grade</em>; the faint line is the running max of
      raw submissions, kept only to show how far apart they are. Each branch draws its raw-max
      curve, then a dotted drop to its re-graded median (diamond); the winner&rsquo;s median is
      where the trajectory steps. Round&nbsp;3 is the lesson: branch b3 logged 602.8 against
      b8&rsquo;s 470.5, but re-graded, b8 wins 453.0 to 450.9. Reset climbs
      327&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;415&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;437&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;453; continue goes
      327&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;335 and stops, keeping its own head at round&nbsp;5 because no branch
      beat it. The baseline lines are re-graded medians too, so trajectory and reference are
      commensurate.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/fig_bsel_cifar.png" alt="fig_bsel_cifar.png"></div>
      <figcaption><b>tinyCIFAR branch selection, retired K=4 snapshot.</b> Axis is merit &mdash; the accuracy gate times
      a log-size term &mdash; because accuracy alone is flat here: essentially every passing solver
      sits at the ~94% bar, so the runs race on size. At about 66.3K credits per arm, reset&rsquo;s
      incumbent was <b>0.496</b>, with a branch at <b>0.500</b>; continuation reached <b>0.480</b>.
      This campaign was stopped on July&nbsp;29 and restarted cleanly with K=8, capped at four
      concurrent branches per arm and balanced across two Copilot credentials. The figure will
      switch to the K=8 campaign once its first fork round lands.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="report-meta-note">&#9679; Caveat on this section&rsquo;s numbers: the grid figures
    above are computed from <em>logged maxima</em> on both sides &mdash; grid cells and native
    baselines alike &mdash; so the comparison is internally consistent, but the levels are not.
    Native INT4 runs inflate <b>1.42&ndash;1.58&times;</b> between logged max and re-graded median
    (&sect;5), which puts the true native median near <b>241</b>, not 356.6. The branch-select
    figures are already on re-graded medians; the grid is not, and is queued for it.</p>
    <p class="report-meta-note">&#9679; data incoming: capped branch-select lineages, matched-credit
    final figures (task&nbsp;#4 / #3b).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s5">
    <h2><span class="n">5</span> Contrast: correlation across agents, induced on purpose</h2>
    <p>
      Communication is the same axis run backwards: a shared board <em>induces</em> correlation
      between agents. On kernel it lifted the whole fleet past the independent best-of-8
      (1070 vs 1097) &mdash; idea-transfer decorrelates each agent from its own rut faster than it
      homogenizes the fleet. On tinyCIFAR it raised the floor but never the ceiling and
      plateaued. Within-agent correlation wastes draws; across-agent correlation can pay &mdash;
      the resource being managed in both cases is the diversity of effective attempts.
    </p>
    <p>
      On INT4 it did neither: <b>communication@4 lost to independent runs</b>, median 129 against
      241, with the best comm agent (162) below six of eight independent runs. Four agents is a
      thin fleet and this is one campaign, so read it as a boundary rather than a reversal &mdash;
      but it is the first substrate where a shared board did not at least raise the floor.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/fig_comm4_int4_trajectories.png" alt="fig_comm4_int4_trajectories.png"></div>
      <figcaption><b>INT4 communication@4 trajectories.</b> <b>Left:</b> per-agent best-so-far on
      a mean-per-agent credit clock. All four comm agents plateau near 150&ndash;180 by ~2,500
      credits and do not move again for the remaining ~9,500, while the independent runs keep
      climbing to 330&ndash;477. They are not merely worse &mdash; they are stuck <em>together</em>,
      which is what a shared board does when it homogenises instead of cross-pollinating.
      <b>Right:</b> iso-cost, K matched at 4 on both sides. The independent average keeps
      single-run cost; both best-of-4 curves pay the aggregate 4-run cost, since a max over four
      runs requires paying for four. Independent best-of-4 dominates comm@4 at every budget.
      Hollow markers are the re-graded medians (310 vs 162) &mdash; the gap between each line&rsquo;s
      end and its marker is the max-inflation, which is why the ranking is stated on medians.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/fig_comm4_int4.png" alt="fig_comm4_int4.png"></div>
      <figcaption><b>INT4 communication@4 vs independent runs, on re-graded medians.</b> Every
      run scored identically: take its best submission, re-grade that artifact 5&times;, drop
      MAD&gt;3 outliers, report the median. <b>Left:</b> logged max (ghost) against what
      reproduces (solid), with the inflation factor above each bar. <b>Right:</b> the two
      populations. This is also the cleanest measurement of max-inflation we have: the eight
      independent runs inflate <b>1.42&ndash;1.58&times;</b> with almost no variation, which is
      what licenses the caveat in &sect;4 and the re-graded baselines in the INT4 branch-select
      figure. Two comm agents inflate only 1.09&ndash;1.13&times; &mdash; they submitted far less,
      and a max over fewer draws is a less biased max.</figcaption>
    </figure>
